1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:10,058
In 1893, a New York lawyer published an adventure book for kids. The main character was a rich boy. Very rich.

2
00:00:10,258 --> 00:00:14,181
He lived in Castle Trump. And his name was Baron Trump.

3
00:00:14,381 --> 00:00:22,073
In the book, he travels to Russia and crosses a portal into a hidden world. He is guided by the writings of a wise man named Don.

4
00:00:22,273 --> 00:00:28,812
More than a century later, in 2017, the internet found the book. And someone did the math.

5
00:00:29,012 --> 00:00:34,319
The president of the United States was named Donald Trump. His youngest son, Barron.

6
00:00:35,269 --> 00:00:38,885
What if the book wasn't a novel? What if it was a message?

7
00:00:39,435 --> 00:00:46,435
This is the story of a theory: that the Trumps are time travelers. And what is actually true behind it.

8
00:00:47,285 --> 00:00:49,515
Chapter 1: The Writer

9
00:00:49,865 --> 00:00:56,712
The author was named Ingersoll Lockwood. He was a lawyer, sometimes a diplomat, and he wrote in his spare time.

10
00:00:56,912 --> 00:01:06,604
He published two books about Baron Trump, in 1889 and 1893. They flopped. And they were forgotten for over a century.

11
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:13,112
The boy in the books is a German nobleman, restless and a know-it-all. He travels with his dog, Bulger.

12
00:01:13,312 --> 00:01:24,619
In 1896 he published something else: a pamphlet called The Last President. It imagines chaos in New York after the election of a candidate the powerful didn't want.

13
00:01:24,819 --> 00:01:29,588
In the pamphlet, an angry mob marches toward a hotel on Fifth Avenue.

14
00:01:30,538 --> 00:01:36,231
Three forgotten books. Until someone read them through the eyes of 2017.

15
00:01:37,081 --> 00:01:39,850
Chapter 2: The Coincidences

16
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:47,969
The internet built the list. A rich boy named Baron Trump. A guide named Don. A journey to Russia.

17
00:01:48,169 --> 00:01:55,246
A last president who wins against everyone. And a mob heading to Fifth Avenue, where Trump Tower stands today.

18
00:01:55,446 --> 00:02:03,985
But let's look closer. The boy in the book is named Baron, with one R. And it's a title of nobility, like count or duke.

19
00:02:04,185 --> 00:02:11,262
Don isn't a first name either. It's the Spanish honorific, like in Don Quixote. The wise man was called Don Fum.

20
00:02:11,462 --> 00:02:19,615
And there was more. The boy in the book is German. And Donald Trump's grandfather arrived in the United States from Germany around that same time.

21
00:02:19,815 --> 00:02:27,431
And the hotel in the pamphlet was on 23rd Street. Trump Tower is on 56th. Thirty-three blocks farther north.

22
00:02:27,631 --> 00:02:34,862
Besides, there is no Baron Trump in The Last President. They are separate books, mashed together later on the internet.

23
00:02:35,812 --> 00:02:39,812
The coincidences are real. But they needed a little nudge.

24
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:43,508
Chapter 3: Millions of Books

25
00:02:43,858 --> 00:02:49,627
Still, how could an 1893 book get so much right? Let's go to the chalkboard.

26
00:02:50,077 --> 00:02:59,923
A quick question. In a room of 23 people, how likely is it that two share the same birthday? It sounds tiny. It's over 50 percent.

27
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:07,354
Because you aren't comparing one person to another. You compare everyone to everyone. That's 253 possible pairs.

28
00:03:07,554 --> 00:03:15,862
Now think about all the books of the 19th century. Thousands of characters with unusual names. And millions of people hunting for matches.

29
00:03:16,062 --> 00:03:25,754
The same thing happens with The Simpsons. In a 2000 episode, Lisa becomes president and inherits the mess of a President Trump. It was a joke about something absurd.

30
00:03:25,954 --> 00:03:33,646
With so many combinations, the strange thing would be finding none. And if you draw the bullseye after shooting the arrow, you never miss.

31
00:03:34,596 --> 00:03:37,750
Out of millions of books, one of them had to hit.

32
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,523
Chapter 4: Tesla's Papers

33
00:03:41,873 --> 00:03:47,181
But the theory still needed a time machine. And it found one in another true story.

34
00:03:47,381 --> 00:03:57,381
In January 1943, inventor Nikola Tesla died in a New York hotel. He was 86 years old and left trunks full of papers.

35
00:03:57,581 --> 00:04:04,581
It was the middle of the war. The U.S. government seized his papers. They feared he had left behind some dangerous weapon.

36
00:04:04,781 --> 00:04:11,704
To review them, they called in an MIT engineer. His name was John Trump. He was Donald Trump's uncle.

37
00:04:11,904 --> 00:04:18,212
His conclusion: they were speculative ideas, with nothing that could be a threat. No machine.

38
00:04:18,412 --> 00:04:27,258
It's true that Tesla had announced astonishing inventions, like a beam capable of knocking planes out of the sky. But nothing working turned up in his papers.

39
00:04:27,458 --> 00:04:36,688
For the theory, it was perfect. Tesla builds the machine and the uncle keeps it. Later, someone in the family travels to the past to leave clues in a book.

40
00:04:37,638 --> 00:04:41,638
The uncle part is true. The machine appears in zero papers.

41
00:04:42,488 --> 00:04:45,642
Chapter 5: How a Theory Is Born

42
00:04:45,992 --> 00:04:52,685
The theory was assembled on forums like Reddit and 4chan in 2017. Each user added a piece.

43
00:04:52,885 --> 00:04:59,962
No single piece was completely fake. The books exist. The uncle existed. Tesla left papers.

44
00:05:00,162 --> 00:05:04,623
What was fake was the glue: the idea that it was all connected.

45
00:05:04,823 --> 00:05:12,823
The funny thing is the books were always right there. They're in the public domain and free to read online. Anyone could check them.

46
00:05:13,023 --> 00:05:20,792
The site Snopes confirmed the books were real. And cleared up the rest: Baron is a title, and Don is too.

47
00:05:20,992 --> 00:05:28,146
Since then, the theory comes back every now and then in short videos with millions of views. Always with the same pieces.

48
00:05:29,096 --> 00:05:33,096
A coincidence doesn't get shared. A conspiracy does.

49
00:05:33,946 --> 00:05:36,715
Chapter 6: Traveling to the Future

50
00:05:37,065 --> 00:05:43,604
Now, the real question. Can you travel in time? To the future, yes. Just a little bit.

51
00:05:44,054 --> 00:05:50,131
To the chalkboard. Over a hundred years ago, Einstein showed that time doesn't pass the same for everyone.

52
00:05:50,331 --> 00:05:58,177
The faster you move, the slower your time passes compared to someone standing still. It's called time dilation.

53
00:05:58,377 --> 00:06:05,992
An astronaut spends six months on the Space Station. They return about five milliseconds younger than if they had stayed.

54
00:06:06,192 --> 00:06:17,192
NASA saw it with twin astronauts. Scott Kelly spent nearly a year in space, while his brother Mark stayed on Earth. Scott came back a few milliseconds younger.

55
00:06:17,392 --> 00:06:26,315
Sounds like a joke, but it's real. GPS satellites correct their clocks for this. Otherwise, your map would be off by miles.

56
00:06:27,265 --> 00:06:31,035
We all travel to the future. At one second per second.

57
00:06:31,885 --> 00:06:34,577
Chapter 7: Traveling to the Past

58
00:06:34,927 --> 00:06:41,542
And to the past, like in the Trump theory? That's where physics gets hard. Last chalkboard.

59
00:06:41,992 --> 00:06:49,069
Imagine you travel to the past and stop your grandparents from meeting. Then you were never born. So who traveled?

60
00:06:49,269 --> 00:06:58,038
That's the grandfather paradox. It's why many physicists think traveling to the past is impossible. Or if you could, you couldn't change a thing.

61
00:06:58,238 --> 00:07:08,315
There are theoretical ideas, like wormholes: shortcuts through space and time. But nobody knows if they exist. And they'd require materials we've never seen.

62
00:07:08,865 --> 00:07:16,250
In 2009, physicist Stephen Hawking ran an experiment. He threw a party for time travelers in Cambridge.

63
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:23,758
The catch: he sent out the invitations after the party. Only someone able to travel to the past could find out in time.

64
00:07:23,958 --> 00:07:26,727
He waited a good while. Nobody showed up.

65
00:07:27,677 --> 00:07:31,292
If traveling to the past were possible, that party would be packed.

66
00:07:32,142 --> 00:07:35,450
Chapter 8: The Book, Again

67
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:41,492
So what is Baron Trump? An adventure book by a lawyer who wanted to be a writer.

68
00:07:41,692 --> 00:07:48,462
It was the era of Jules Verne, and fantastic underground journeys were in fashion. Lockwood wrote his own.

69
00:07:48,662 --> 00:07:56,585
Lockwood died in 1918. He never knew that, a century later, his failed books would go viral.

70
00:07:56,785 --> 00:08:06,862
The coincidences are real. But they're explained by math, by titles that looked like names, by three books mixed together, and by a strong desire to find something.

71
00:08:07,062 --> 00:08:14,369
And time travel? To the future, we all travel slowly. To the past, so far, nobody has arrived.

72
00:08:14,569 --> 00:08:19,108
Hawking's invitation is still out there. It has a date, a time, and a place.

73
00:08:20,058 --> 00:08:24,519
If someday someone travels to the past, they already know where a party is waiting.

74
00:08:25,469 --> 00:08:31,623
What historical mystery or theory would you like us to investigate in the next episode? Let us know in the comments.
